{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1793383395704,"sessionID":"ses_e72053da9ffeY5Vp446Zu901pu","part":{"type":"tool","tool":"read","callID":"call_01a18dfaec457788a6f348c074c04c98","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"filePath":"/kaggle/tmp/AI-Scientist-v3-main/results/revisit/opencode/20261030_163231/investigation/worker_02/ideas/10_geometry_diverse_constructive_growth_for_six_hour_revisit_compression/prompt.md"},"output":"<path>/kaggle/tmp/AI-Scientist-v3-main/results/revisit/opencode/20261030_163231/investigation/worker_02/ideas/10_geometry_diverse_constructive_growth_for_six_hour_revisit_compression/prompt.md</path>\n<type>file</type>\n<content>\n1: # Role\n2: You are an autonomous scientist/engineer running an iterative optimization campaign.\n3: Goal: improve objective score while preserving correctness and satisfying rubrics.\n4: Use `edit-program`, `evaluate-program`, `verify-rubrics`, and `memory-log` skill workflows for execution.\n5: \n6: # Task Context\n7: ## Goal\n8: Design a small Earth-observation constellation and its photo plan. A mission has 24-32 ground targets and lasts 48 hours.\n9: Every target must be photographed again and again: the longest time without a photo (the \"revisit gap\") must stay at or\n10: below the target's revisit requirement (6 or 8 hours). You choose how many satellites to launch (at most `cap`, 16-20),\n11: the orbit of each satellite, and which photos each satellite takes. Fewer satellites are better, but only once every\n12: target meets its requirement. Write `solve(world, time_limit_s)` that returns the satellites and the photo plan.\n13: The score is measured on 4 development missions; the final evaluation re-runs the program on sealed missions of the same\n14: kind that are not on this machine, so the method must work on new missions, not on these 4 only.\n15: \n16: # Active Idea\n17: Attempt ID: `cycle_10_geometry_diverse_constructive_growth_for_six_hour_revisit_compression_attempt_01`\n18: Idea ID: `cycle_10_geometry_diverse_constructive_growth_for_six_hour_revisit_compression`\n19: \n20: Idea bundle (`./active_idea.json`):\n21: ```json\n22: {\n23:   \"hypothesis\": \"The 58-59 satellite plateau across cycles 4-9 is bound by orbit coverage geometry, not scheduler ordering, removal ordering, sampled pool enrichment, joint replacement, or in-window photo timing, all of which left tight 6h fleets at 16-17. Growing a fleet bottom-up from geometry-diverse Walker families that vary plane count/distribution, inter-plane phasing, RAAN offset, inclination, and altitude, with each addition judged by the actual slew-audited cached scheduler's edge-inclusive gap violation, will find a differently-shaped feasible geometry that pruned cap-Walker fleets cannot reach, cutting each 6h fleet to at most 15 and the 4-mission total to at most 55 while keeping all targets feasible, all plans valid, and max solve time at most 60s.\",\n24:   \"idea_id\": \"cycle_10_geometry_diverse_constructive_growth_for_six_hour_revisit_compression\",\n25:   \"implementation_plan\": [\n26:     \"Fork the best-manifest program (cycle-09 window-flexible slew-aware, 77.0263) and retain unchanged its correct off-nadir geometry, verified non-empty cap-sized seed stored immediately as best-so-far, single cached fast propagation for the seed, slew-safe insertion, final slew audit, and requirement-exact edge-inclusive gap verification; never return empty actions and use no exact propagation inside search loops.\",\n27:     \"After the seed is stored, generate a bounded set of geometry-diverse Walker families (at most 4) that systematically vary plane count and satellites-per-plane distribution, Walker phasing factor and RAAN offset, and one inclination and one altitude offset from the seed geometry; derive family parameters only from world targets/requirements/cap so the method generalises to sealed missions.\",\n28:     \"Score each family cheaply with the existing cached scheduler at a reduced fleet size using total edge-inclusive requirement-normalised gap violation (not a coverage proxy), keep only the best 1-2 families, and propagate each kept family at most once with fast propagation, caching all candidate passes for reuse.\",\n29:     \"Construct the candidate fleet bottom-up: repeatedly add the single satellite (or whole under-filled plane when a plane is opened) whose cached candidates most reduce the scheduler-verified total gap violation, re-running the slew-audited scheduler after each addition, and stop as soon as every target meets its 6h/8h requirement including mission edges.\",\n30:     \"Compress the grown fleet with the existing verified single-satellite pruning (accept only after a full cached re-schedule plus slew/settling/battery audit), then return the smaller of the grown fleet and the seed fleet, both fully verified.\",\n31:     \"Enforce a hard internal deadline near 70% of time_limit_s with deadline checks inside family scoring, propagation, growth, and pruning loops; on expiry return the best verified fleet found so far.\"\n32:   ],\n33:   \"notes_for_agent\": \"Fork the best-manifest program at ideas/09_window_flexible_slew_aware_scheduling_to_break_the_tight_fleet_plateau/program_1 and keep its seed-first, slew-audited, cached pipeline intact; the only new mechanism is geometry-diverse constructive growth after the seed. Do not revisit exonerated directions: window-time offsets (cycle 9, identical 58), scheduler-only retuning or removal orderings (cycles 4/6, identical 59), sampled representative enrichment or sequential single swap+prune (cycle 7, identical 59), joint pair replacement (cycle 8, regressed to 60), displacement repair (cycle 5, slew-invalid), req-0.3h margin acceptance (cycle 6 fast-only, total 68), or unchecked exact propagation in loops (98-154s maxima in cycles 5/7/8). Test a tight 20-satellite 6h mission first: confirm the verified seed is stored before any family propagation, the grown fleet reaches at most 15 satellites with the slew audit passing, and solve time stays well under 60s; then check an 8h mission, validate per-case n_satellites/n_targets_missing/worst_gap_h/n_actions and max_solve_s, and run the full evaluation once. Never return empty actions; on any deadline or failure return the best verified fleet found.\",\n34:   \"target_rubrics\": [\n35:     {\n36:       \"description\": \"combined_score strictly exceeds 77.4211 (the best score recorded in cycles 4-9) on the 4 development missions\",\n37:       \"id\": \"combined_score_beats_best\",\n38:       \"type\": \"metric\"\n39:     },\n40:     {\n41:       \"description\": \"Every mission has n_targets_missing=0 and worst_gap_h at or below each target's 6h/8h revisit requirement including mission edges\",\n42:       \"id\": \"all_missions_feasible_including_tight\",\n43:       \"type\": \"metric\"\n44:     },\n45:     {\n46:       \"description\": \"Sum of n_satellites over the 4 development missions is at most 55, versus 58-59 in cycles 4-9\",\n47:       \"id\": \"total_fleet_at_most_55\",\n48:       \"type\": \"metric\"\n49:     },\n50:     {\n51:       \"description\": \"Each tight 6h-requirement mission uses at most 15 satellites, versus 16-17 in cycles 4-9\",\n52:       \"id\": \"tight_six_hour_fleets_at_most_15\",\n53:       \"type\": \"metric\"\n54:     },\n55:     {\n56:       \"description\": \"All 4 missions are valid (n_valid=4) with no slew, settling, battery, overlap, or off-nadir violations and non-empty action lists\",\n57:       \"id\": \"valid_plan_no_violations\",\n58:       \"type\": \"metric\"\n59:     },\n60:     {\n61:       \"description\": \"max_solve_s is at most 60 seconds across the 4 development missions with no degenerate or timeout returns\",\n62:       \"id\": \"runtime_at_most_60s\",\n63:       \"type\": \"metric\"\n64:     },\n65:     {\n66:       \"description\": \"A verified non-empty cap-sized seed is scheduled and stored as best-so-far before any geometry search, every insertion is slew/settling/battery checked against predecessor and successor, and a final slew audit passes before any fleet is accepted or returned\",\n67:       \"id\": \"seed_first_slew_audited_fallback_retained\",\n68:       \"type\": \"implementation\"\n69:     },\n70:     {\n71:       \"description\": \"At most 4 Walker families varying plane distribution, phasing/RAAN offset, inclination, and altitude are scored by the actual scheduler's edge-inclusive gap violation, and the fleet is grown bottom-up by verified marginal gap reduction rather than only pruned from the cap Walker fleet\",\n72:       \"id\": \"geometry_diverse_constructive_growth\",\n73:       \"type\": \"implementation\"\n74:     },\n75:     {\n76:       \"description\": \"Each kept geometry is fast-propagated at most once with candidates cached and reused for scoring, growth, and pruning; no exact propagation runs inside search loops and a hard deadline near 70% of time_limit_s is checked inside every costly loop\",\n77:       \"id\": \"cached_fast_only_deadline_enforced_search\",\n78:       \"type\": \"implementation\"\n79:     },\n80:     {\n81:       \"description\": \"Geometry families, growth, and stopping decisions are derived only from the supplied world (targets, requirements, cap, time limit) with no hardcoded mission ids, case indices, target coordinates, or dev-mission fleet sizes, so the method transfers to sealed missions\",\n82:       \"id\": \"generalizes_without_hardcoding\",\n83:       \"type\": \"implementation\"\n84:     }\n85:   ],\n86:   \"title\": \"Geometry-Diverse Constructive Growth for Six-Hour Revisit Compression\"\n87: }\n88: ```\n89: \n90: Target rubric bundle (`./active_rubrics.json`):\n91: ```json\n92: [\n93:   {\n94:     \"description\": \"combined_score strictly exceeds 77.4211 (the best score recorded in cycles 4-9) on the 4 development missions\",\n95:     \"id\": \"combined_score_beats_best\",\n96:     \"type\": \"metric\"\n97:   },\n98:   {\n99:     \"description\": \"Every mission has n_targets_missing=0 and worst_gap_h at or below each target's 6h/8h revisit requirement including mission edges\",\n100:     \"id\": \"all_missions_feasible_including_tight\",\n101:     \"type\": \"metric\"\n102:   },\n103:   {\n104:     \"description\": \"Sum of n_satellites over the 4 development missions is at most 55, versus 58-59 in cycles 4-9\",\n105:     \"id\": \"total_fleet_at_most_55\",\n106:     \"type\": \"metric\"\n107:   },\n108:   {\n109:     \"description\": \"Each tight 6h-requirement mission uses at most 15 satellites, versus 16-17 in cycles 4-9\",\n110:     \"id\": \"tight_six_hour_fleets_at_most_15\",\n111:     \"type\": \"metric\"\n112:   },\n113:   {\n114:     \"description\": \"All 4 missions are valid (n_valid=4) with no slew, settling, battery, overlap, or off-nadir violations and non-empty action lists\",\n115:     \"id\": \"valid_plan_no_violations\",\n116:     \"type\": \"metric\"\n117:   },\n118:   {\n119:     \"description\": \"max_solve_s is at most 60 seconds across the 4 development missions with no degenerate or timeout returns\",\n120:     \"id\": \"runtime_at_most_60s\",\n121:     \"type\": \"metric\"\n122:   },\n123:   {\n124:     \"description\": \"A verified non-empty cap-sized seed is scheduled and stored as best-so-far before any geometry search, every insertion is slew/settling/battery checked against predecessor and successor, and a final slew audit passes before any fleet is accepted or returned\",\n125:     \"id\": \"seed_first_slew_audited_fallback_retained\",\n126:     \"type\": \"implementation\"\n127:   },\n128:   {\n129:     \"description\": \"At most 4 Walker families varying plane distribution, phasing/RAAN offset, inclination, and altitude are scored by the actual scheduler's edge-inclusive gap violation, and the fleet is grown bottom-up by verified marginal gap reduction rather than only pruned from the cap Walker fleet\",\n130:     \"id\": \"geometry_diverse_constructive_growth\",\n131:     \"type\": \"implementation\"\n132:   },\n133:   {\n134:     \"description\": \"Each kept geometry is fast-propagated at most once with candidates cached and reused for scoring, growth, and pruning; no exact propagation runs inside search loops and a hard deadline near 70% of time_limit_s is checked inside every costly loop\",\n135:     \"id\": \"cached_fast_only_deadline_enforced_search\",\n136:     \"type\": \"implementation\"\n137:   },\n138:   {\n139:     \"description\": \"Geometry families, growth, and stopping decisions are derived only from the supplied world (targets, requirements, cap, time limit) with no hardcoded mission ids, case indices, target coordinates, or dev-mission fleet sizes, so the method transfers to sealed missions\",\n140:     \"id\": \"generalizes_without_hardcoding\",\n141:     \"type\": \"implementation\"\n142:   }\n143: ]\n144: ```\n145: \n146: # Overall Pipeline\n147: `edit-program -> evaluate-program -> verify-rubrics`\n148: \n149: For each iteration:\n150: 1. `edit-program`: create next variant at `./program_<idx>/` (never overwrite old dirs). Code lives in `program_<idx>/src/**` and metadata in `program_<idx>/program_spec.toml`.\n151: 2. `evaluate-program`: run `./evaluate.py` and collect `metrics.json`, `correct.json`, logs.\n152: 3. `verify-rubrics`: use the `verify-rubrics` skill workflow to check rubric compliance with evidence.\n153: 4. Immediately update `./memory.md` using `memory-log` with a structured evidence entry for this iteration.\n154: 5. Immediately write per-iteration rubric artifact to `./program_<idx>/rubric_status.json`.\n155: 6. Update `./rubric_status.json` to match the latest evaluated `program_<idx>` status.\n156: 7. Only then decide next change from evidence.\n157: \n158: # Hard Iteration Controls\n159: - Always run `evaluate-program -> verify-rubrics` as one atomic pair for each `program_<idx>`.\n160: - Never start editing `program_<idx+1>` before writing rubric artifacts for `program_<idx>`.\n161: - Use `src.investigation.program_clone` for every candidate clone.\n162: - Never use `cp`, `cp -r`, `cp -a`, `rsync`, or another recursive copy to create a candidate.\n163: - Stop the attempt immediately when latest `rubric_status.json` has `overall_pass: true`.\n164: - Evaluation budget is based on calls, not folders: each command that runs `evaluate.py` consumes one evaluation, including `program_0`, retries, failed runs, overwrites, and repeated runs on the same `program_<idx>`.\n165: - You may make at most `1` total `evaluate.py` calls in a single attempt.\n166: - Do not evaluate `program_0` unless explicitly required by the task. Treat `./program_0/` as the read-only implementation reference and use existing baseline artifacts if present.\n167: - If `1` is `1`, create exactly one edited candidate (`program_1`), run `evaluate.py` exactly once on `program_1`, then verify rubrics, write memory, and stop if rubrics fail.\n168: - Do not re-run `evaluate.py` for the same program. Use the existing result, inspect logs if needed, verify rubrics from available evidence, write memory, and stop with `cap_reached`.\n169: - If the final allowed evaluation call fails any rubric or the evaluation itself fails, stop the attempt and report cap reached.\n170: \n171: # No Exploratory Execution\n172: - Do not run exploratory computation, timing probes, benchmarks, optimizer searches, ad hoc metric scripts, or local experiments outside the official `evaluate.py` call.\n173: - Do not run `python -c`, heredoc Python, notebooks, `pip install`, `pip`, `pytest`, `time`, SciPy optimizers, differential evolution, SLSQP probes, or direct task-function calls.\n174: - Before evaluation, use file reads and edits only. After evaluation, inspect only the artifacts and logs produced by that official evaluation.\n175: - The only allowed Python execution is the single official `evaluate.py` command, subject to the evaluation budget.\n176: \n177: # Evaluation Runtime\n178: - Use the task runtime for every official evaluator call so the evaluator and candidate retain the task's installed dependencies:\n179: \n180: ```bash\n181: \"${TASK_PYTHON:-$WRAPPER_PYTHON}\" -I -m src.investigation.evaluate_program \\\n182:     --evaluator \"./evaluate.py\" \\\n183:     --program-dir \"./program_<idx>\" \\\n184:     --timeout-seconds \"${EVALUATION_TIMEOUT_SECONDS:-21600}\"\n185: ```\n186: \n187: - Always use this wrapper command; do not invoke `evaluate.py` directly. Evaluator-owned logs and subdirectories stay outside the candidate tree, while validated `metrics.json` and `correct.json` summaries are published at the program root.\n188: - Do not wrap the command with the shell `timeout` utility. If the shell tool supports an unlimited/omitted timeout, omit its optional timeout. Otherwise, configure it longer than the effective evaluator timeout plus its cleanup margin. Never choose a shell-tool timeout shorter than the evaluator timeout.\n189: - Never invoke `evaluate.py` with bare `python`, `python3`, or its shebang. The container's wrapper interpreter is intentionally separate from the task runtime and may not contain task dependencies.\n190: - Candidate code must use only the task runtime's installed dependencies. Never add user-site or absolute `site-packages` paths, and never hard-code paths to the agent home or container filesystem.\n191: \n192: # Memory Protocol\n193: - Continuously update `./memory.md` (create it if missing; append-only, never erase prior entries).\n194: - Keep this local file attempt-scoped in the current attempt directory (`ideas/<idea>/<attempt>/`), not as a campaign-global file.\n195: - Also append every major-step memory event to:\n196:   - `../../memory.md`\n197:   - `../../memory.jsonl`\n198: - At least one structured event for this attempt must include `attempt_id` exactly equal to `cycle_10_geometry_diverse_constructive_growth_for_six_hour_revisit_compression_attempt_01`.\n199: - At least one event about each evaluated program must include `program_id` exactly equal to `program_<idx>` on the same record as that `attempt_id`; do not substitute fields such as `attempt` or `best_program`.\n200: - Treat `../../memory.md` as the single campaign-level markdown memory file.\n201: - `../../memory.md` must live in the same results-root directory as `outer_state.json`; never create `memory.md` under `ideas/` subdirectories.\n202: - Use `../../memory.jsonl` as the authoritative cross-idea memory log.\n203: - Start from this compact recent-memory summary before reading more from disk:\n204: - item 1: step=idea; idea_id=cycle_09_window_time_scheduler_capacity; summary=Cycle 9 idea Window-Time Optimised Scheduler for Tight-Revisit Fleet Compression: fork cycle-8 best, keep seed-first cached slew-safe pipeline, union-pool constructive minimisation and joint compression, but give each cached pass up to 3 start-time offsets (earliest-safe/centre/latest-safe, same single propagation) chosen by predecessor/successor slew/settling/battery feasibility and residual slack, plus a bounded slew-audited re-timing pass sliding photos only within their own pass and retrying rejected critical-gap candidates once. gate=kept; trigger=none; reason=idea-stage hypothesis with no new evaluation evidence, so no distilled-conclusion update is warranted.; next_action=Implement solve() per idea_bundle.json on top of the cycle-8 best program; test a tight 20-satellite 6h mission first, confirm seed stored before offset/pool work and a 6h fleet below 16 with slew audit passing, validate per-case n_satellites/n_targets_missing/worst_gap_h/n_actions and max_solve_s, then run full evaluation once; do not re-evaluate cycle-8 program_1.\n205: - item 2: step=final; idea_id=cycle_09_window_time_scheduler_capacity; summary=idea_evaluation_summary: program_1 window-time scheduler scored 77.4211 correct=true, 8/10 rubrics, stop cap_reached; valid/feasible, max_solve_s=50.28, but total fleet 58 >55 (13/16/16/13) so score and deeper-fleet rubrics fail; window-time freedom exonerated for tight 6h fleets. gate=updated; trigger=d; reason=refines scheduler-capacity conclusion, narrowing future effort to orbit geometry.; next_action=Fresh budget only: change Walker plane/phasing geometry for tight 6h missions; do not re-evaluate program_1\n206: - item 3: step=idea; idea_id=cycle_09_window_flexible_slew_aware_scheduling_to_break_tight_fleet_plateau; summary=Cycle 9 idea Window-Flexible Slew-Aware Scheduling to Break the Tight Fleet Plateau: fork cycle-3 best, keep verified non-empty cap seed and cached fast propagation, use multiple interior candidate instants per visibility window chosen by slew/slack cost plus bounded slew-safe time-shift of neighbours, requirement-exact verified pruning, no exact propagation in solve(), 50% hard deadline. gate=kept; trigger=none; reason=untested idea-stage hypothesis applying recorded cycle-8 next actions, no new evaluation evidence.; next_action=Implement solve() per idea_bundle.json on top of cycle-3 best; test tight 6h first for early verified seed, slew-audited window-flexible scheduling and fleet below 16, then 8h, validate per-case n_satellites/n_targets_missing/worst_gap_h/n_actions and max_solve_s, then full evaluation once; never return empty actions.\n207: - item 4: step=final; idea_id=cycle_09_window_flexible_slew_aware_scheduling_to_break_tight_fleet_plateau; summary=idea_evaluation_summary: program_1 window-flexible slew-aware scored 77.0263 correct=true, 8/10 rubrics, stop cap_reached; all valid/feasible max_solve_s=28.03 but total 59>55 and 6h fleets 17/17 so score/fleet rubrics fail. gate=kept; trigger=none; reason=regression vs 77.42 confirms fixed timing not binding plateau.; next_action=Fresh budget only: pair window-flexible scheduler with diverse orbit geometries/constructive growth for 6h; do not re-evaluate program_1\n208: - item 5: step=idea; idea_id=cycle_10_geometry_diverse_constructive_growth_for_six_hour_revisit_compression; summary=Cycle 10 idea Geometry-Diverse Constructive Growth for Six-Hour Revisit Compression: fork best-manifest cycle-9 window-flexible slew-aware program, retain verified non-empty seed-first cached pipeline with slew audit and requirement-exact edge-inclusive verification, then after the seed score at most 4 geometry-diverse Walker families (plane distribution, phasing/RAAN offset, inclination, altitude) by the actual scheduler gap violation, fast-propagate only the best 1-2, grow a fleet bottom-up by verified marginal gap reduction until feasible, compress with verified pruning, return the smaller verified fleet under a ~70% hard deadline. gate=kept; trigger=none; reason=untested idea-stage hypothesis applying recorded cycle-9 next actions, no new evaluation evidence, so no distilled-conclusion update is warranted.; next_action=Implement solve() per idea_bundle.json on top of the best-manifest program; test a tight 20-satellite 6h mission first for early verified seed and grown fleet at most 15 with slew audit passing, then an 8h mission, validate per-case n_satellites/n_targets_missing/worst_gap_h/n_actions and max_solve_s, then run full evaluation once; never return empty actions.\n209: - After every `evaluate-program -> verify-rubrics` pair, append one `Program Evaluations` entry with:\n210:   - `program_<idx>`\n211:   - `combined_score`\n212:   - `correct`\n213:   - rubric pass/fail summary\n214:   - next recommendation\n215: - Log key discoveries/decisions for each major step (`edit`, `evaluate`, `verify`) before moving to the next step.\n216: - At attempt end (stop reason `rubric_pass` or `cap_reached`), migrate relevant findings from local `./memory.md` into global memory:\n217:   - append one `idea_evaluation_summary` entry to `../../memory.md`\n218:   - append one matching structured event to `../../memory.jsonl`\n219:   - include: best `program_<idx>`, best metrics (`combined_score`, `correct`), final rubric status summary, key failures/learnings, and next recommendation\n220:   - summarize/highlight only (do not copy-paste the full local memory file)\n221: \n222: # Start Point\n223: - Reference baseline project: `./program_0/` (read-only baseline copy).\n224: - Entrypoint is defined by `program_0/program_spec.toml` (default: `src/program.py`).\n225: - Start by creating `./program_1/` from `program_0`, then evaluate `program_1` exactly once.\n226: - Do not run a baseline `program_0` evaluation unless the task explicitly requires it; if you do, it consumes one `evaluate.py` call from the same budget.\n227: \n228: # Operating Rules\n229: - Never ask user questions.\n230: - If info missing, make best reasonable assumption and continue.\n231: - Prefer root-cause fixes; avoid random search edits.\n232: - Keep reasoning brief and concrete.\n233: - Do not chain edits without rubric verification for the just-evaluated program.\n234: - Before any command containing `evaluate.py`, check whether the total evaluation budget has already been consumed. If no budget remains, do not run the command.\n235: \n236: # Required Artifacts\n237: After **every** evaluation of `program_<idx>`, write `./program_<idx>/rubric_status.json`.\n238: Before exit, ensure `./rubric_status.json` exists and exactly matches the latest evaluated program rubric artifact.\n239: \n240: Artifact structure:\n241: \n242: ```json\n243: {\n244:   \"attempt_id\": \"cycle_10_geometry_diverse_constructive_growth_for_six_hour_revisit_compression_attempt_01\",\n245:   \"idea_id\": \"cycle_10_geometry_diverse_constructive_growth_for_six_hour_revisit_compression\",\n246:   \"overall_pass\": false,\n247:   \"rubrics\": [\n248:     {\"id\": \"rubric_id\", \"pass\": false, \"evidence\": \"brief evidence\"}\n249:   ],\n250:   \"metrics\": {\"combined_score\": 0.0, \"correct\": false},\n251:   \"next_recommendation\": \"short next step\"\n252: }\n253: ```\n254: \n255: # Exit Checklist\n256: 1. Identify latest evaluated index `<idx>`.\n257: 2. Confirm `program_<idx>/rubric_status.json` exists.\n258: 3. Confirm `./rubric_status.json` equals `program_<idx>/rubric_status.json`.\n259: 4. Confirm latest `program_<idx>` evidence is logged in `./memory.md`.\n260: 5. Confirm relevant local-memory findings were migrated to global memory (`../../memory.md` and `../../memory.jsonl`).\n261: 6. Confirm global JSONL contains one event with `attempt_id` exactly equal to `cycle_10_geometry_diverse_constructive_growth_for_six_hour_revisit_compression_attempt_01` and `program_id` exactly equal to the latest `program_<idx>` on the same record.\n262: 7. Confirm no more than `1` total `evaluate.py` calls were made, including any `program_0` evaluation.\n263: 8. Confirm stop reason is one of: `rubric_pass` or `cap_reached`.\n\n(End of file - total 263 lines)\n</content>","metadata":{"preview":"# Role\nYou are an autonomous scientist/engineer running an iterative optimization campaign.\nGoal: improve objective score while preserving correctness and satisfying rubrics.\nUse `edit-program`, `evaluate-program`, `verify-rubrics`, and `memory-log` skill workflows for execution.\n\n# Task Context\n## Goal\nDesign a small Earth-observation constellation and its photo plan. A mission has 24-32 ground targets and lasts 48 hours.\nEvery target must be photographed again and again: the longest time without a photo (the \"revisit gap\") must stay at or\nbelow the target's revisit requirement (6 or 8 hours). You choose how many satellites to launch (at most `cap`, 16-20),\nthe orbit of each satellite, and which photos each satellite takes. Fewer satellites are better, but only once every\ntarget meets its requirement. Write `solve(world, time_limit_s)` that returns the satellites and the photo plan.\nThe score is measured on 4 development missions; the final evaluation re-runs the program on sealed missions of the same\nkind that are not on this machine, so the method must work on new missions, not on these 4 only.\n\n# Active Idea\nAttempt ID: `cycle_10_geometry_diverse_constructive_growth_for_six_hour_revisit_compression_attempt_01`\nIdea ID: `cycle_10_geometry_diverse_constructive_growth_for_six_hour_revisit_compression`\n\nIdea bundle (`./active_idea.json`):","truncated":false,"loaded":[],"display":{"type":"file","path":"/kaggle/tmp/AI-Scientist-v3-main/results/revisit/opencode/20261030_163231/investigation/worker_02/ideas/10_geometry_diverse_constructive_growth_for_six_hour_revisit_compression/prompt.md","text":"# Role\nYou are an autonomous scientist/engineer running an iterative optimization campaign.\nGoal: improve objective score while preserving correctness and satisfying rubrics.\nUse `edit-program`, `evaluate-program`, `verify-rubrics`, and `memory-log` skill workflows for execution.\n\n# Task Context\n## Goal\nDesign a small Earth-observation constellation and its photo plan. A mission has 24-32 ground targets and lasts 48 hours.\nEvery target must be photographed again and again: the longest time without a photo (the \"revisit gap\") must stay at or\nbelow the target's revisit requirement (6 or 8 hours). You choose how many satellites to launch (at most `cap`, 16-20),\nthe orbit of each satellite, and which photos each satellite takes. Fewer satellites are better, but only once every\ntarget meets its requirement. Write `solve(world, time_limit_s)` that returns the satellites and the photo plan.\nThe score is measured on 4 development missions; the final evaluation re-runs the program on sealed missions of the same\nkind that are not on this machine, so the method must work on new missions, not on these 4 only.\n\n# Active Idea\nAttempt ID: `cycle_10_geometry_diverse_constructive_growth_for_six_hour_revisit_compression_attempt_01`\nIdea ID: `cycle_10_geometry_diverse_constructive_growth_for_six_hour_revisit_compression`\n\nIdea bundle (`./active_idea.json`):\n```json\n{\n  \"hypothesis\": \"The 58-59 satellite plateau across cycles 4-9 is bound by orbit coverage geometry, not scheduler ordering, removal ordering, sampled pool enrichment, joint replacement, or in-window photo timing, all of which left tight 6h fleets at 16-17. Growing a fleet bottom-up from geometry-diverse Walker families that vary plane count/distribution, inter-plane phasing, RAAN offset, inclination, and altitude, with each addition judged by the actual slew-audited cached scheduler's edge-inclusive gap violation, will find a differently-shaped feasible geometry that pruned cap-Walker fleets cannot reach, cutting each 6h fleet to at most 15 and the 4-mission total to at most 55 while keeping all targets feasible, all plans valid, and max solve time at most 60s.\",\n  \"idea_id\": \"cycle_10_geometry_diverse_constructive_growth_for_six_hour_revisit_compression\",\n  \"implementation_plan\": [\n    \"Fork the best-manifest program (cycle-09 window-flexible slew-aware, 77.0263) and retain unchanged its correct off-nadir geometry, verified non-empty cap-sized seed stored immediately as best-so-far, single cached fast propagation for the seed, slew-safe insertion, final slew audit, and requirement-exact edge-inclusive gap verification; never return empty actions and use no exact propagation inside search loops.\",\n    \"After the seed is stored, generate a bounded set of geometry-diverse Walker families (at most 4) that systematically vary plane count and satellites-per-plane distribution, Walker phasing factor and RAAN offset, and one inclination and one altitude offset from the seed geometry; derive family parameters only from world targets/requirements/cap so the method generalises to sealed missions.\",\n    \"Score each family cheaply with the existing cached scheduler at a reduced fleet size using total edge-inclusive requirement-normalised gap violation (not a coverage proxy), keep only the best 1-2 families, and propagate each kept family at most once with fast propagation, caching all candidate passes for reuse.\",\n    \"Construct the candidate fleet bottom-up: repeatedly add the single satellite (or whole under-filled plane when a plane is opened) whose cached candidates most reduce the scheduler-verified total gap violation, re-running the slew-audited scheduler after each addition, and stop as soon as every target meets its 6h/8h requirement including mission edges.\",\n    \"Compress the grown fleet with the existing verified single-satellite pruning (accept only after a full cached re-schedule plus slew/settling/battery audit), then return the smaller of the grown fleet and the seed fleet, both fully verified.\",\n    \"Enforce a hard internal deadline near 70% of time_limit_s with deadline checks inside family scoring, propagation, growth, and pruning loops; on expiry return the best verified fleet found so far.\"\n  ],\n  \"notes_for_agent\": \"Fork the best-manifest program at ideas/09_window_flexible_slew_aware_scheduling_to_break_the_tight_fleet_plateau/program_1 and keep its seed-first, slew-audited, cached pipeline intact; the only new mechanism is geometry-diverse constructive growth after the seed. Do not revisit exonerated directions: window-time offsets (cycle 9, identical 58), scheduler-only retuning or removal orderings (cycles 4/6, identical 59), sampled representative enrichment or sequential single swap+prune (cycle 7, identical 59), joint pair replacement (cycle 8, regressed to 60), displacement repair (cycle 5, slew-invalid), req-0.3h margin acceptance (cycle 6 fast-only, total 68), or unchecked exact propagation in loops (98-154s maxima in cycles 5/7/8). Test a tight 20-satellite 6h mission first: confirm the verified seed is stored before any family propagation, the grown fleet reaches at most 15 satellites with the slew audit passing, and solve time stays well under 60s; then check an 8h mission, validate per-case n_satellites/n_targets_missing/worst_gap_h/n_actions and max_solve_s, and run the full evaluation once. Never return empty actions; on any deadline or failure return the best verified fleet found.\",\n  \"target_rubrics\": [\n    {\n      \"description\": \"combined_score strictly exceeds 77.4211 (the best score recorded in cycles 4-9) on the 4 development missions\",\n      \"id\": \"combined_score_beats_best\",\n      \"type\": \"metric\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"description\": \"Every mission has n_targets_missing=0 and worst_gap_h at or below each target's 6h/8h revisit requirement including mission edges\",\n      \"id\": \"all_missions_feasible_including_tight\",\n      \"type\": \"metric\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"description\": \"Sum of n_satellites over the 4 development missions is at most 55, versus 58-59 in cycles 4-9\",\n      \"id\": \"total_fleet_at_most_55\",\n      \"type\": \"metric\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"description\": \"Each tight 6h-requirement mission uses at most 15 satellites, versus 16-17 in cycles 4-9\",\n      \"id\": \"tight_six_hour_fleets_at_most_15\",\n      \"type\": \"metric\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"description\": \"All 4 missions are valid (n_valid=4) with no slew, settling, battery, overlap, or off-nadir violations and non-empty action lists\",\n      \"id\": \"valid_plan_no_violations\",\n      \"type\": \"metric\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"description\": \"max_solve_s is at most 60 seconds across the 4 development missions with no degenerate or timeout returns\",\n      \"id\": \"runtime_at_most_60s\",\n      \"type\": \"metric\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"description\": \"A verified non-empty cap-sized seed is scheduled and stored as best-so-far before any geometry search, every insertion is slew/settling/battery checked against predecessor and successor, and a final slew audit passes before any fleet is accepted or returned\",\n      \"id\": \"seed_first_slew_audited_fallback_retained\",\n      \"type\": \"implementation\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"description\": \"At most 4 Walker families varying plane distribution, phasing/RAAN offset, inclination, and altitude are scored by the actual scheduler's edge-inclusive gap violation, and the fleet is grown bottom-up by verified marginal gap reduction rather than only pruned from the cap Walker fleet\",\n      \"id\": \"geometry_diverse_constructive_growth\",\n      \"type\": \"implementation\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"description\": \"Each kept geometry is fast-propagated at most once with candidates cached and reused for scoring, growth, and pruning; no exact propagation runs inside search loops and a hard deadline near 70% of time_limit_s is checked inside every costly loop\",\n      \"id\": \"cached_fast_only_deadline_enforced_search\",\n      \"type\": \"implementation\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"description\": \"Geometry families, growth, and stopping decisions are derived only from the supplied world (targets, requirements, cap, time limit) with no hardcoded mission ids, case indices, target coordinates, or dev-mission fleet sizes, so the method transfers to sealed missions\",\n      \"id\": \"generalizes_without_hardcoding\",\n      \"type\": \"implementation\"\n    }\n  ],\n  \"title\": \"Geometry-Diverse Constructive Growth for Six-Hour Revisit Compression\"\n}\n```\n\nTarget rubric bundle (`./active_rubrics.json`):\n```json\n[\n  {\n    \"description\": \"combined_score strictly exceeds 77.4211 (the best score recorded in cycles 4-9) on the 4 development missions\",\n    \"id\": \"combined_score_beats_best\",\n    \"type\": \"metric\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"description\": \"Every mission has n_targets_missing=0 and worst_gap_h at or below each target's 6h/8h revisit requirement including mission edges\",\n    \"id\": \"all_missions_feasible_including_tight\",\n    \"type\": \"metric\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"description\": \"Sum of n_satellites over the 4 development missions is at most 55, versus 58-59 in cycles 4-9\",\n    \"id\": \"total_fleet_at_most_55\",\n    \"type\": \"metric\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"description\": \"Each tight 6h-requirement mission uses at most 15 satellites, versus 16-17 in cycles 4-9\",\n    \"id\": \"tight_six_hour_fleets_at_most_15\",\n    \"type\": \"metric\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"description\": \"All 4 missions are valid (n_valid=4) with no slew, settling, battery, overlap, or off-nadir violations and non-empty action lists\",\n    \"id\": \"valid_plan_no_violations\",\n    \"type\": \"metric\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"description\": \"max_solve_s is at most 60 seconds across the 4 development missions with no degenerate or timeout returns\",\n    \"id\": \"runtime_at_most_60s\",\n    \"type\": \"metric\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"description\": \"A verified non-empty cap-sized seed is scheduled and stored as best-so-far before any geometry search, every insertion is slew/settling/battery checked against predecessor and successor, and a final slew audit passes before any fleet is accepted or returned\",\n    \"id\": \"seed_first_slew_audited_fallback_retained\",\n    \"type\": \"implementation\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"description\": \"At most 4 Walker families varying plane distribution, phasing/RAAN offset, inclination, and altitude are scored by the actual scheduler's edge-inclusive gap violation, and the fleet is grown bottom-up by verified marginal gap reduction rather than only pruned from the cap Walker fleet\",\n    \"id\": \"geometry_diverse_constructive_growth\",\n    \"type\": \"implementation\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"description\": \"Each kept geometry is fast-propagated at most once with candidates cached and reused for scoring, growth, and pruning; no exact propagation runs inside search loops and a hard deadline near 70% of time_limit_s is checked inside every costly loop\",\n    \"id\": \"cached_fast_only_deadline_enforced_search\",\n    \"type\": \"implementation\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"description\": \"Geometry families, growth, and stopping decisions are derived only from the supplied world (targets, requirements, cap, time limit) with no hardcoded mission ids, case indices, target coordinates, or dev-mission fleet sizes, so the method transfers to sealed missions\",\n    \"id\": \"generalizes_without_hardcoding\",\n    \"type\": \"implementation\"\n  }\n]\n```\n\n# Overall Pipeline\n`edit-program -> evaluate-program -> verify-rubrics`\n\nFor each iteration:\n1. `edit-program`: create next variant at `./program_<idx>/` (never overwrite old dirs). Code lives in `program_<idx>/src/**` and metadata in `program_<idx>/program_spec.toml`.\n2. `evaluate-program`: run `./evaluate.py` and collect `metrics.json`, `correct.json`, logs.\n3. `verify-rubrics`: use the `verify-rubrics` skill workflow to check rubric compliance with evidence.\n4. Immediately update `./memory.md` using `memory-log` with a structured evidence entry for this iteration.\n5. Immediately write per-iteration rubric artifact to `./program_<idx>/rubric_status.json`.\n6. Update `./rubric_status.json` to match the latest evaluated `program_<idx>` status.\n7. Only then decide next change from evidence.\n\n# Hard Iteration Controls\n- Always run `evaluate-program -> verify-rubrics` as one atomic pair for each `program_<idx>`.\n- Never start editing `program_<idx+1>` before writing rubric artifacts for `program_<idx>`.\n- Use `src.investigation.program_clone` for every candidate clone.\n- Never use `cp`, `cp -r`, `cp -a`, `rsync`, or another recursive copy to create a candidate.\n- Stop the attempt immediately when latest `rubric_status.json` has `overall_pass: true`.\n- Evaluation budget is based on calls, not folders: each command that runs `evaluate.py` consumes one evaluation, including `program_0`, retries, failed runs, overwrites, and repeated runs on the same `program_<idx>`.\n- You may make at most `1` total `evaluate.py` calls in a single attempt.\n- Do not evaluate `program_0` unless explicitly required by the task. Treat `./program_0/` as the read-only implementation reference and use existing baseline artifacts if present.\n- If `1` is `1`, create exactly one edited candidate (`program_1`), run `evaluate.py` exactly once on `program_1`, then verify rubrics, write memory, and stop if rubrics fail.\n- Do not re-run `evaluate.py` for the same program. Use the existing result, inspect logs if needed, verify rubrics from available evidence, write memory, and stop with `cap_reached`.\n- If the final allowed evaluation call fails any rubric or the evaluation itself fails, stop the attempt and report cap reached.\n\n# No Exploratory Execution\n- Do not run exploratory computation, timing probes, benchmarks, optimizer searches, ad hoc metric scripts, or local experiments outside the official `evaluate.py` call.\n- Do not run `python -c`, heredoc Python, notebooks, `pip install`, `pip`, `pytest`, `time`, SciPy optimizers, differential evolution, SLSQP probes, or direct task-function calls.\n- Before evaluation, use file reads and edits only. After evaluation, inspect only the artifacts and logs produced by that official evaluation.\n- The only allowed Python execution is the single official `evaluate.py` command, subject to the evaluation budget.\n\n# Evaluation Runtime\n- Use the task runtime for every official evaluator call so the evaluator and candidate retain the task's installed dependencies:\n\n```bash\n\"${TASK_PYTHON:-$WRAPPER_PYTHON}\" -I -m src.investigation.evaluate_program \\\n    --evaluator \"./evaluate.py\" \\\n    --program-dir \"./program_<idx>\" \\\n    --timeout-seconds \"${EVALUATION_TIMEOUT_SECONDS:-21600}\"\n```\n\n- Always use this wrapper command; do not invoke `evaluate.py` directly. Evaluator-owned logs and subdirectories stay outside the candidate tree, while validated `metrics.json` and `correct.json` summaries are published at the program root.\n- Do not wrap the command with the shell `timeout` utility. If the shell tool supports an unlimited/omitted timeout, omit its optional timeout. Otherwise, configure it longer than the effective evaluator timeout plus its cleanup margin. Never choose a shell-tool timeout shorter than the evaluator timeout.\n- Never invoke `evaluate.py` with bare `python`, `python3`, or its shebang. The container's wrapper interpreter is intentionally separate from the task runtime and may not contain task dependencies.\n- Candidate code must use only the task runtime's installed dependencies. Never add user-site or absolute `site-packages` paths, and never hard-code paths to the agent home or container filesystem.\n\n# Memory Protocol\n- Continuously update `./memory.md` (create it if missing; append-only, never erase prior entries).\n- Keep this local file attempt-scoped in the current attempt directory (`ideas/<idea>/<attempt>/`), not as a campaign-global file.\n- Also append every major-step memory event to:\n  - `../../memory.md`\n  - `../../memory.jsonl`\n- At least one structured event for this attempt must include `attempt_id` exactly equal to `cycle_10_geometry_diverse_constructive_growth_for_six_hour_revisit_compression_attempt_01`.\n- At least one event about each evaluated program must include `program_id` exactly equal to `program_<idx>` on the same record as that `attempt_id`; do not substitute fields such as `attempt` or `best_program`.\n- Treat `../../memory.md` as the single campaign-level markdown memory file.\n- `../../memory.md` must live in the same results-root directory as `outer_state.json`; never create `memory.md` under `ideas/` subdirectories.\n- Use `../../memory.jsonl` as the authoritative cross-idea memory log.\n- Start from this compact recent-memory summary before reading more from disk:\n- item 1: step=idea; idea_id=cycle_09_window_time_scheduler_capacity; summary=Cycle 9 idea Window-Time Optimised Scheduler for Tight-Revisit Fleet Compression: fork cycle-8 best, keep seed-first cached slew-safe pipeline, union-pool constructive minimisation and joint compression, but give each cached pass up to 3 start-time offsets (earliest-safe/centre/latest-safe, same single propagation) chosen by predecessor/successor slew/settling/battery feasibility and residual slack, plus a bounded slew-audited re-timing pass sliding photos only within their own pass and retrying rejected critical-gap candidates once. gate=kept; trigger=none; reason=idea-stage hypothesis with no new evaluation evidence, so no distilled-conclusion update is warranted.; next_action=Implement solve() per idea_bundle.json on top of the cycle-8 best program; test a tight 20-satellite 6h mission first, confirm seed stored before offset/pool work and a 6h fleet below 16 with slew audit passing, validate per-case n_satellites/n_targets_missing/worst_gap_h/n_actions and max_solve_s, then run full evaluation once; do not re-evaluate cycle-8 program_1.\n- item 2: step=final; idea_id=cycle_09_window_time_scheduler_capacity; summary=idea_evaluation_summary: program_1 window-time scheduler scored 77.4211 correct=true, 8/10 rubrics, stop cap_reached; valid/feasible, max_solve_s=50.28, but total fleet 58 >55 (13/16/16/13) so score and deeper-fleet rubrics fail; window-time freedom exonerated for tight 6h fleets. gate=updated; trigger=d; reason=refines scheduler-capacity conclusion, narrowing future effort to orbit geometry.; next_action=Fresh budget only: change Walker plane/phasing geometry for tight 6h missions; do not re-evaluate program_1\n- item 3: step=idea; idea_id=cycle_09_window_flexible_slew_aware_scheduling_to_break_tight_fleet_plateau; summary=Cycle 9 idea Window-Flexible Slew-Aware Scheduling to Break the Tight Fleet Plateau: fork cycle-3 best, keep verified non-empty cap seed and cached fast propagation, use multiple interior candidate instants per visibility window chosen by slew/slack cost plus bounded slew-safe time-shift of neighbours, requirement-exact verified pruning, no exact propagation in solve(), 50% hard deadline. gate=kept; trigger=none; reason=untested idea-stage hypothesis applying recorded cycle-8 next actions, no new evaluation evidence.; next_action=Implement solve() per idea_bundle.json on top of cycle-3 best; test tight 6h first for early verified seed, slew-audited window-flexible scheduling and fleet below 16, then 8h, validate per-case n_satellites/n_targets_missing/worst_gap_h/n_actions and max_solve_s, then full evaluation once; never return empty actions.\n- item 4: step=final; idea_id=cycle_09_window_flexible_slew_aware_scheduling_to_break_tight_fleet_plateau; summary=idea_evaluation_summary: program_1 window-flexible slew-aware scored 77.0263 correct=true, 8/10 rubrics, stop cap_reached; all valid/feasible max_solve_s=28.03 but total 59>55 and 6h fleets 17/17 so score/fleet rubrics fail. gate=kept; trigger=none; reason=regression vs 77.42 confirms fixed timing not binding plateau.; next_action=Fresh budget only: pair window-flexible scheduler with diverse orbit geometries/constructive growth for 6h; do not re-evaluate program_1\n- item 5: step=idea; idea_id=cycle_10_geometry_diverse_constructive_growth_for_six_hour_revisit_compression; summary=Cycle 10 idea Geometry-Diverse Constructive Growth for Six-Hour Revisit Compression: fork best-manifest cycle-9 window-flexible slew-aware program, retain verified non-empty seed-first cached pipeline with slew audit and requirement-exact edge-inclusive verification, then after the seed score at most 4 geometry-diverse Walker families (plane distribution, phasing/RAAN offset, inclination, altitude) by the actual scheduler gap violation, fast-propagate only the best 1-2, grow a fleet bottom-up by verified marginal gap reduction until feasible, compress with verified pruning, return the smaller verified fleet under a ~70% hard deadline. gate=kept; trigger=none; reason=untested idea-stage hypothesis applying recorded cycle-9 next actions, no new evaluation evidence, so no distilled-conclusion update is warranted.; next_action=Implement solve() per idea_bundle.json on top of the best-manifest program; test a tight 20-satellite 6h mission first for early verified seed and grown fleet at most 15 with slew audit passing, then an 8h mission, validate per-case n_satellites/n_targets_missing/worst_gap_h/n_actions and max_solve_s, then run full evaluation once; never return empty actions.\n- After every `evaluate-program -> verify-rubrics` pair, append one `Program Evaluations` entry with:\n  - `program_<idx>`\n  - `combined_score`\n  - `correct`\n  - rubric pass/fail summary\n  - next recommendation\n- Log key discoveries/decisions for each major step (`edit`, `evaluate`, `verify`) before moving to the next step.\n- At attempt end (stop reason `rubric_pass` or `cap_reached`), migrate relevant findings from local `./memory.md` into global memory:\n  - append one `idea_evaluation_summary` entry to `../../memory.md`\n  - append one matching structured event to `../../memory.jsonl`\n  - include: best `program_<idx>`, best metrics (`combined_score`, `correct`), final rubric status summary, key failures/learnings, and next recommendation\n  - summarize/highlight only (do not copy-paste the full local memory file)\n\n# Start Point\n- Reference baseline project: `./program_0/` (read-only baseline copy).\n- Entrypoint is defined by `program_0/program_spec.toml` (default: `src/program.py`).\n- Start by creating `./program_1/` from `program_0`, then evaluate `program_1` exactly once.\n- Do not run a baseline `program_0` evaluation unless the task explicitly requires it; if you do, it consumes one `evaluate.py` call from the same budget.\n\n# Operating Rules\n- Never ask user questions.\n- If info missing, make best reasonable assumption and continue.\n- Prefer root-cause fixes; avoid random search edits.\n- Keep reasoning brief and concrete.\n- Do not chain edits without rubric verification for the just-evaluated program.\n- Before any command containing `evaluate.py`, check whether the total evaluation budget has already been consumed. If no budget remains, do not run the command.\n\n# Required Artifacts\nAfter **every** evaluation of `program_<idx>`, write `./program_<idx>/rubric_status.json`.\nBefore exit, ensure `./rubric_status.json` exists and exactly matches the latest evaluated program rubric artifact.\n\nArtifact structure:\n\n```json\n{\n  \"attempt_id\": \"cycle_10_geometry_diverse_constructive_growth_for_six_hour_revisit_compression_attempt_01\",\n  \"idea_id\": \"cycle_10_geometry_diverse_constructive_growth_for_six_hour_revisit_compression\",\n  \"overall_pass\": false,\n  \"rubrics\": [\n    {\"id\": \"rubric_id\", \"pass\": false, \"evidence\": \"brief evidence\"}\n  ],\n  \"metrics\": {\"combined_score\": 0.0, \"correct\": false},\n  \"next_recommendation\": \"short next step\"\n}\n```\n\n# Exit Checklist\n1. Identify latest evaluated index `<idx>`.\n2. Confirm `program_<idx>/rubric_status.json` exists.\n3. Confirm `./rubric_status.json` equals `program_<idx>/rubric_status.json`.\n4. Confirm latest `program_<idx>` evidence is logged in `./memory.md`.\n5. Confirm relevant local-memory findings were migrated to global memory (`../../memory.md` and `../../memory.jsonl`).\n6. Confirm global JSONL contains one event with `attempt_id` exactly equal to `cycle_10_geometry_diverse_constructive_growth_for_six_hour_revisit_compression_attempt_01` and `program_id` exactly equal to the latest `program_<idx>` on the same record.\n7. Confirm no more than `1` total `evaluate.py` calls were made, including any `program_0` evaluation.\n8. Confirm stop reason is one of: `rubric_pass` or `cap_reached`.","lineStart":1,"lineEnd":263,"totalLines":263,"truncated":false}},"title":"results/revisit/opencode/20261030_163231/investigation/worker_02/ideas/10_geometry_diverse_constructive_growth_for_six_hour_revisit_compression/prompt.md","time":{"start":1793383394849,"end":1793383395595}},"metadata":{"openai":{"itemId":"fc_01a18dfaec457788a6f348c074c04c98"}},"id":"prt_18dfaed80001qGzPHB5nBAxYF0","sessionID":"ses_e72053da9ffeY5Vp446Zu901pu","messageID":"msg_18dfad099001Qf8EJ1gJFiX4w6"}}
